      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the efficacy of intravenous versus oral aspirin use in patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "ACS patients were randomly treated with intravenous aspirin (300 mg/d, IA, n = 30), low oral aspirin (100 mg/d, OA1, n = 32) or high oral aspirin (300 mg/d, OA2, n = 33). Aspirin sensitivity was tested by optical platelet aggregation using adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and arachidonic acid (AA). The serum CD62p contents were examined by Flow cytometry."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Platelet aggregation expressed as ratio of reduction of ADP and AA post various aspirin were similar among 3 groups [IA: ADP (12.0 +/- 10.4)%, AA (6.7 +/- 11.2)%; OA1: ADP (6.0 +/- 14.6)%, AA (6.9 +/- 12.3)%; OA2: ADP (9.4 +/- 16.6)%, AA (7.3 +/- 13.0)%, all P > 0.05]. CD62p decreasing level post various aspirin were also similar among groups [IA: (10.9 +/- 18.6)%, OA1: (9.0 +/- 11.8)%, OA2: (7.1 +/- 15.7)%, all P > 0.05]. Side-effects and MACE post various aspirin use were comparable among groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Inhibiting efficacy on platelets function by intravenous aspirin (300 mg/d) was comparable to that of by oral aspirin (100 mg/d, 300 mg/d) in patients with acute coronary syndrome and could be used as an alternative route for patients who can't take oral aspirin."
        }
      ]},
